        <history>
          <p>Composed during the First World War, the Fourth Symphony carries the subtitle ‘The Inextinguishable’, reflecting Nielsen's conviction that music expresses ‘the elemental will to live’. He wrote that the symphony should suggest what he called “the life force, the unquenchable will to live”, a response to the violence of the war years. Its dramatic use of two timpani players in physical opposition near the close is one of the most striking gestures in the early twentieth-century symphonic repertoire, and the work remains Nielsen's most widely performed orchestral piece internationally.</p>
